<!--
  /compare — SUI vs React, Svelte, Solid. Honest, confident. The table
  maps the major axes a developer chooses between; the prose focuses
  on what is unique to SUI and where the others win.
-->
<div class="doc compare-doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guides</p>
  <h1>Compare</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI is a compiler-first reactive framework. React, Svelte, and Solid
    are mature, well-loved tools — and they each ship trade-offs SUI
    does not. The table below maps the axes a developer chooses
    between; the prose focuses on what is unique to SUI and where the
    other three still win.
  </p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="compare-table-wrap">
    <table class="compare-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th>SUI</th><th>React</th><th>Svelte 5</th><th>Solid</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Compile step</th>
          <td>AOT to web components</td>
          <td>Optional (Babel / SWC)</td>
          <td>AOT to JS</td>
          <td>Optional (Babel / SWC)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Reactivity</th>
          <td>Fine-grained signals, derived, effect</td>
          <td>Re-render on state change</td>
          <td>Fine-grained runes</td>
          <td>Fine-grained signals</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>State</th>
          <td>Signal — read/write a function</td>
          <td>useState / useReducer</td>
          <td>$state runes</td>
          <td>createSignal</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Output unit</th>
          <td>Custom element (Shadow DOM)</td>
          <td>Virtual DOM in JS</td>
          <td>Direct DOM ops</td>
          <td>Direct DOM ops</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Component model</th>
          <td>.sui + controller pair, custom element</td>
          <td>JSX + hooks, virtual tree</td>
          <td>.svelte + script, compile to DOM</td>
          <td>JSX + signals, compile to DOM</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Data binding</th>
          <td>Built-in <code>&lt;data&gt;</code> seam, loading + error envelopes</td>
          <td>Hand-rolled (React Query, SWR, etc.)</td>
          <td>Hand-rolled (stores / context)</td>
          <td>Hand-rolled (createResource)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Agent tooling</th>
          <td>LSP + browser LSP + CLI, one grammar</td>
          <td>TS server (community)</td>
          <td>TS server (community)</td>
          <td>TS server (community)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Styling</th>
          <td>Inline <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> + tokens, shadow-scoped</td>
          <td>CSS Modules / Tailwind / CSS-in-JS</td>
          <td>Inline <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> + tokens, scoped</td>
          <td>CSS Modules / CSS-in-JS</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Distribution</th>
          <td>Custom elements; framework-agnostic host</td>
          <td>React-DOM only</td>
          <td>Compiled bundle</td>
          <td>Compiled bundle</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Where SUI wins</h2>
  <dl class="wins">
    <dt>Compiler-first output</dt>
    <dd>
      SUI templates compile AOT to small, fast web components. The output
      unit is a custom element with its own shadow root — no React-DOM
      equivalent to ship, no JSX runtime, no virtual tree. The same
      <code>my-card</code> component renders in a SUI page, in a static
      HTML page, in another framework, or in a Storybook story.
    </dd>
    <dt>Live data, built in</dt>
    <dd>
      The <code>&lt;data&gt;</code> directive binds a remote source
      straight into a view as a signal — loading and error envelopes
      model the network for you, with a single <code>live</code> flag
      that turns the result into a stream. Other frameworks leave this
      to the ecosystem (React Query, TanStack Query, SWR, Solid
      Resource). SUI ships the seam; the ecosystem is a
      <code>source=</code> string away.
    </dd>
    <dt>One grammar, three transports</dt>
    <dd>
      SUI ships a single Rust grammar that powers the CLI, the LSP, and
      the browser-side analyzer. The same diagnostic shape reaches an
      agent's stderr, an editor's gutter, and a docs page's lint
      indicators. Agents and humans get the same answers.
    </dd>
    <dt>Native shadow scoping</dt>
    <dd>
      Inline <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> blocks compile into the
      component's shadow root. No class-name collisions, no
      CSS-in-JS runtime, no cascade to debug. The token API keeps
      themes working without a build-time pre-processor.
    </dd>
    <dt>Frame-agnostic output</dt>
    <dd>
      Because the output is a custom element, a SUI component drops into
      any web platform: server-rendered HTML, a non-SUI SPA, a static
      page, an MDX doc, an iframe. No vendor lock-in past
      <code>customElements</code>.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Where the others win</h2>
  <dl class="wins">
    <dt>React</dt>
    <dd>
      The ecosystem. TanStack Query, Mantine, MUI, Framer Motion,
      RSC, server components, and a decade of stack-overflow answers.
      If the answer must exist, React has it.
    </dd>
    <dt>Svelte 5</dt>
    <dd>
      Compile-to-DOM with a single-file <code>.svelte</code> format
      that has its own templating, stores, and motion primitives
      already wired. The footprint and the per-component granularity
      are the smallest of the four.
    </dd>
    <dt>Solid</dt>
    <dd>
      The most React-like JSX API of the three fine-grained frameworks
      and a JSX that compiles to direct DOM ops with no virtual tree.
      Its signal model is the closest reference for SUI's.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>The honest trade</h2>
  <p>
    SUI gives up the ambient React-DOM / VDOM assumption in exchange for
    custom elements. That is a real cost: any tool that assumes a
    React tree (most of the React ecosystem) needs an adapter, and the
    SPA-style "client owns the whole DOM" pattern is not the natural
    fit. The pay-off is that the same component ships anywhere a
    custom element can mount, that data binding is a one-liner, and
    that an LSP-driven agent tool loop is first-class.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the rest of your stack is React, stay there. If you're building
    a docs site, a design system, an embeddable widget, an agent
    tool, or anything that needs to land in someone else's page —
    SUI is the right fit.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/tooling">← Tooling</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/tutorial">Take the tutorial →</a>
  </div>
</div>
